<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Breiddin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Breiddin Hill</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Breiddin is a steep volcanic hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a> in Powys, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, close to the English border. Its summit carries a large hillfort first fortified in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, occupied through much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and used again in the late Roman period. Excavations have made it one of the best-studied hillforts in the territory of the Cornovii.</p></div>
